<p>A group of Massachusetts investors organized the {% place "mandan-land-and-development-company" %}Mandan Land and Development Company{% endplace %} and established a flour mill in 1885. This “Boston Syndicate” invested heavily in southwest Mandan, a district still known as the {% place "syndicate-section" %}Syndicate{% endplace %}. During its history, the facility operated as the Mandan Roller Mill, North Dakota Millers' Association, Missouri Valley Milling Company, and Russell-Miller Milling Company.</p>

<p>The Syndicate arranged for a rail spur to the {% place "heart-river" %}Heart River{% endplace %}. After completing a brickyard and building-supply warehouse, it laid foundations for a mill producing 200 barrels of flour per day and an accompanying grain elevator. Brick from the company's own yard was used in construction. The site drew water and power from the Heart River and had transportation access through its {% place "northern-pacific-railroad" %}Northern Pacific{% endplace %} rail spur. It became a major Mandan employer for more than 50 years and was among the first local businesses to generate its own electricity.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Mr. E.E. Watson has just completed a big job of lettering the roof of the Mandan Roller Mill Company’s warehouse near the mill. The words ‘Mandan Roller Mill Company’ are painted in white letters twelve feet high and stretch a distance of 210 feet. They are well proportioned and look bold and prominent from the railroad tracks.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>—{% place "mandan-pioneer" %}<em>The Mandan Pioneer</em>{% endplace %}, October 1887</p>

<p>In 1888, the mill contracted to provide federal Indian agencies across the Northwest with 1.3 million pounds of flour. By 1898, its capacity had reached 600 barrels per day, making it North Dakota's largest flour mill.</p>

<p>Several changes came in 1891. A wooden-barrel shop opened in August, construction began on a warehouse for more than 2,000 barrels and a 50,000-bushel grain elevator, and the mill was sold to the {% place "north-dakota-milling-association" %}North Dakota Millers' Association{% endplace %}, a group of state banks and investors. By 1897, former owners and banks had filed lawsuits over unpaid loans.</p>

