<p>In 1899, {% person "hiram-rogers-lyon" %}Hiram Lyon{% endperson %}, mill manager {% person "benjamin-washburn-stephenson" %}Benjamin Stephenson{% endperson %}, and {% person "john-henry-newton" %}John Newton{% endperson %} purchased the facility and organized the {% place "missouri-valley-milling-company" %}Missouri Valley Milling Company{% endplace %}. Lyon served as president, Newton as secretary, and Stephenson as treasurer. The company trademarked its premium flour as Lyon's Best, while Climax became its best-selling brand.</p>

<p>The company strongly influenced industrial development in the Missouri Slope. Its investment grew from {% inflation 50000, 1885 %} to {% inflation 500000, 1909 %}. The Mandan plant produced 500 barrels per day, while mills in Bismarck and Dickinson brought total output to 1,200 barrels. Its flour won the grand prize at the 1904 St. Louis World's Fair.</p>

<p>A major fire on April 1, 1902, caused {% inflation 1500, 1902 %} in damage, but the mill was rebuilt and upgraded. In June 1909, regional firm {% place "russell-miller-milling-company" %}Russell-Miller Milling Company{% endplace %} purchased the company's three mills and 17 grain elevators to meet demand for its Occident flour. The operations at the three North Dakota flour mills were upgraded. Hiram Lyon became a corporate vice president, Newton became auditor, and Stephenson became western area manager. Russell-Miller, which began with operations in Valley City and later Jamestown, eventually grew into the nation's fourth-largest flour-milling operation. North Dakota plants continued promoting Climax flour and their association with the former Missouri Valley company.</p>

